      \field{abstract}{The symmetry of the superconducting order parameter, or simply the ‘‘gap’’, provides certain constraints on the actual mechanism that gives rise to pairing and ultimately to superconductivity. In this work we show how superconducting phases with mixed singlet-triplet symmetries can arise below 𝑇𝑐 for a generic tight-binding model. We first examine the 1D case to better illustrate the prevalence of symmetry-breaking transitions below 𝑇𝑐 , and then the more realistic 2D case. In both cases we illustrate the implication for spectroscopic investigations of the energy gap by calculating the density of states for different temperatures below 𝑇𝑐 . We find that the structure of the density of states near 𝑇𝑐 can vary dramatically from its form near 𝑇 = 0. A complete picture of the superconducting symmetry can only be attained if measurements are made over the entire temperature range.}
